{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Multimedia Storyboarding and AI Asset Generation","title":"Peer Review of Storyboards","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting peer review of storyboards to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-01","statement":"Diagnose why a storyboard peer comment fails by identifying which of the three properties (anchored, actionable, authorship-preserving) is missing"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-02","statement":"Rewrite a praise or taste comment into a three-part review (observation, anchor, option) that cites a Mayer principle or the frame objective"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-03","statement":"Explain why the author's documented accept-or-reject response, not the comment itself, is the design evidence, and why a principled rejection can be stronger than an acceptance"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-04","statement":"Maintain anchored, authorship-preserving review across a power gradient, as in the senior-to-junior transfer case"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation","peer_review","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"07","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/03/06"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_artifact_signal"],"ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Peer Review of Storyboards\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Peer Review, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/03-07-peer-review-of-storyboards.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Peer Review of Storyboards: Peer Review, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\nMost peer feedback on storyboards is useless, and predictably so. Left unstructured, reviewers default to two failure modes: vague praise (\"looks great, nice colors\") that gives the author nothing to act on, and personal taste (\"I would have used blue\") that the shared design criteria do not support. Neither tells the author whether a frame teaches, and both quietly assert the reviewer's preferences as if they were standards. Effective peer review is a learnable discipline with a structure, not a personality trait of generous people.\n\nThis section treats peer review as criteria-anchored, actionable, and authorship-preserving. Anchored means every comment cites a shared principle (a Mayer principle, the frame's stated objective, a load concern) rather than the reviewer's preference. Actionable means the author is left with a concrete decision, not a compliment. Authorship-preserving means the reviewer surfaces the issue and may offer an option, but the revision choice, and the accountability for it, stays with the author. The design evidence produced is not the comment alone; it is the author's recorded accept-or-reject decision and the reason behind it.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Diagnose why a storyboard peer comment fails by identifying which of the three properties (anchored, actionable, authorship-preserving) is missing.\n- Rewrite a praise or taste comment into a three-part review (observation, anchor, option) that cites a Mayer principle or the frame objective.\n- Explain why the author's documented accept-or-reject response, not the comment itself, is the design evidence, and why a principled rejection can be stronger than an acceptance.\n- Maintain anchored, authorship-preserving review across a power gradient, as in the senior-to-junior transfer case.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA classmate shares a storyboard whose Frame 4 packs three diagrams and a full paragraph onto one screen. The first reviewer writes \"Looks great! I love the colors, though I'd have used a blue background.\" The author reads it, feels mildly complimented, and changes nothing meaningful, because there is nothing to change: the comment names no problem, cites no principle, and offers only a preference the assignment criteria do not support.\n\nThe real issue, that Frame 4 is overloaded and competes for the visual channel, went unnamed. The comment was friendly and worthless. This section asks you to expose what separates that comment from a review: a review names the principle at stake, points to the specific frame and objective, and hands the author a decision rather than a verdict on taste.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA useful storyboard review comment has three properties, and dropping any one of them produces a recognizable failure.\n\n- **Anchored.** It cites a shared standard, a Mayer principle, the frame's own stated objective, or a load concern, not the reviewer's preference. Anchoring is what gives the author grounds to weigh the comment instead of obeying or dismissing it. The opposite, taste (\"use blue\"), has no warrant.\n- **Actionable.** It points to a specific frame and names a concrete change the author could make (\"move the paragraph to narration; split the third diagram to its own frame\"). The opposite, vague praise, gives nothing to do.\n- **Authorship-preserving.** It surfaces the issue and may offer options, but leaves the revision decision to the author. The opposite, prescription (\"change it to this\"), seizes the author's design ownership and short-circuits their judgment.\n\nThe deeper move is recognizing what counts as the design evidence. It is not the reviewer's comment; it is the **author's documented response**, the accept-or-reject decision and its reason. A comment that is accepted without reasoning, or rejected with \"I disagree,\" produces no evidence. A comment that the author rejects with a principled reason (\"I kept both diagrams side by side because the objective is to compare them\") produces strong evidence of judgment. These map to the course anchors directly: Mayer's coherence and segmenting principles and [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load) supply the standards a comment anchors to, and UDL learner variability is often the basis for an option a reviewer offers (\"this works for fluent readers; what about the rest?\"). The anchor must change a decision; a comment that names a principle but would not alter the frame is decoration, not review.\n\nMatch each property of a useful review comment to the recognizable failure that appears when you drop it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Anchored\",\"definition\":\"Drop it and you get taste (I would have used blue): a preference with no shared warrant the author can weigh\"},{\"term\":\"Actionable\",\"definition\":\"Drop it and you get vague praise (looks great): nothing concrete the author can do\"},{\"term\":\"Authorship-preserving\",\"definition\":\"Drop it and you get prescription (change it to this): the reviewer seizes the author’s design ownership\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n\n## Worked Example: Rewriting a Peer Comment Into Real Review\n\nA classmate shares a storyboard whose Frame 4 packs three diagrams and a paragraph onto one screen. Below is the before/after of the peer comment.\n\n**Before (praise and taste, not review):**\n\n> \"Looks great! I love the colors. I would have used a blue background though.\"\n\nProblems: general praise plus a personal-preference suggestion. Nothing names a principle, points to the real issue (Frame 4 is overloaded), or gives the author a decision to make.\n\n**After (anchored, actionable, authorship preserved):**\n\n> \"Frame 4 may exceed the coherence and segmenting principles: three diagrams plus a full paragraph compete for the visual channel. The learning objective for this frame is to compare two diagrams, so the third diagram and the paragraph may be extraneous. Option: move the paragraph to narration and split the third diagram to its own frame. Your call on which fits your sequence.\"\n\nAnnotated decision trail:\n\n1. Replaced praise with a specific observation tied to a named principle (coherence, segmenting) and the frame's objective.\n2. Dropped the personal-taste color comment, which the shared criteria do not support.\n3. Offered an option but left the revision choice to the author, so review surfaces the issue without seizing authorship.\n4. Author's response recorded: accepted moving the paragraph to narration, rejected splitting the diagram (the comparison needs both side by side), and noted why. That accept/reject record is the design evidence.\n\nThe improvement is feedback that is actionable and criteria-anchored, and an authoring decision the author owns.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWrite every review comment in a fixed three-part shape, observation, anchor, option, and refuse to send one that is missing a part:\n\n1. **Observation:** State what you see on a specific frame, factually (\"Frame 4 has three diagrams and a full paragraph on one screen\").\n2. **Anchor:** Tie it to a shared standard (\"this may exceed the coherence and segmenting principles, and the frame's objective is only to compare two diagrams\").\n3. **Option, not order:** Offer a possible move and return the decision (\"you could move the paragraph to narration; your call on what fits the sequence\").\n\nStrip anything that is pure taste (\"I'd use blue\") unless the criteria support it. Then, as the author receiving review, complete the loop: record each comment as accepted or rejected with a reason, because that response, not the comment, is the design evidence the assignment is really collecting.\n\nWalk the overloaded Frame 4 review from comment to authoring decision.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"From peer comment to documented decision\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Frame 4 packs three diagrams and a full paragraph onto one screen. What is the most useful comment to write?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Looks great, though I would have used a blue background\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Pure praise plus taste. It names no principle, no real issue, and gives the author nothing to decide.\"},{\"label\":\"Frame 4 may exceed coherence and segmenting; the objective is only to compare two diagrams, so the third diagram and paragraph may be extraneous. Option: move the paragraph to narration; your call.\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Anchored to a principle and the objective, actionable, and it returns the decision to the author.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"The author changes nothing meaningful. What was missing?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"An anchor to a shared standard and a concrete, optional move\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right: tone was never the problem; anchoring and actionability were.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Friendly and worthless: no principle, no decision.\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"The author now responds. She accepts moving the paragraph to narration but rejects splitting the two diagrams because the objective is to compare them side by side. Which response is the stronger design evidence?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The acceptance, because acting on feedback shows responsiveness\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Accepting all feedback is abdication, not judgment. A quiet acceptance shows less reasoning than a principled rejection.\"},{\"label\":\"The principled rejection, because it weighs the comment against the objective\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: the reasoned rejection shows the author decided rather than complied.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Why is a reasoned rejection often stronger than an acceptance?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It shows the author weighed the comment against the frame objective instead of obeying\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Right: that weighing is the judgment the assignment collects.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Responsiveness is not the same as judgment.\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"What exactly is the design evidence here?\",\"feedback\":\"Not the comment, but the author’s documented accept-or-reject decision and its reason tied to the objective. A single anchored comment produced two pieces of evidence, and the reasoned rejection is the stronger one.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is that good review means being either nice or harsh. Tone is not the variable; anchoring is. A warm comment with no anchor is as useless as a blunt one, and a blunt comment that cites the objective is valuable. The second misreading is that the author should act on every comment. Accepting all feedback is not responsiveness; it is abdication. A strong author rejects comments that conflict with the objective and records why, and that principled rejection is stronger evidence of design judgment than a quiet acceptance.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A reviewer writes: 'Looks great! I love the colors, though I'd have used a blue background.' Which property of a useful comment is missing first?\" explanation=\"The comment is unanchored: it cites no shared standard, only personal taste the assignment criteria do not support. It also names no real issue (Frame 4 is overloaded) and gives no actionable decision, but the root failure is that it rests on preference rather than a principle or the frame's objective.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Nothing is missing; warm feedback is good review\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is unanchored: it cites taste rather than a shared standard, so the author has no grounds to weigh it\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is too harsh and should be softened\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk the overloaded Frame 4 through the shape. Observation: \"Frame 4 carries three diagrams and a paragraph competing for the visual channel.\" Anchor: \"the objective is to compare two diagrams, so the third diagram and the paragraph may be extraneous (coherence, segmenting).\" Option: \"you could move the paragraph to narration and split the third diagram onto its own frame, but choose what fits your sequence.\" Now the author's response becomes the evidence: she accepts moving the paragraph to narration (it was duplicating the diagram's point) and rejects splitting the diagrams (the comparison needs both side by side, which is the objective), and records both reasons.\n\nNotice the division of labor. The reviewer surfaced a real, anchored problem and offered a route; the author made and owned two opposite decisions, one accept and one reject, each justified by the objective. The walkthrough shows that a single well-formed comment can produce two pieces of design evidence, and that the author's reasoned rejection is the stronger of the two.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead peer comments by testing each for the three parts and by asking \"what could the author actually do with this?\" For each comment in the chapter's examples, identify the observation, the anchor, and whether it preserves authorship or seizes it. Then look past the comment to the author's response and judge the reasoning, not the choice; an accept and a reject can both be excellent if both cite the objective. A strong reader can spot the comment that is really a preference wearing the costume of feedback.\n\nA strong reader of peer review can, for any exchange, name the standard the comment anchors to, the decision it leaves to the author, and the reason behind the author's accept-or-reject response.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact reaches the stronger standard when it pairs anchored, option-style comments with the author's documented responses, including at least one comment the author accepted and one the author rejected, each with a reason tied to the frame's objective. The trace should show a comment being declined on principle, because a review record with only acceptances suggests the author was complying, not deciding. Pure-taste comments, if present, are marked as out of scope against the criteria.\n\nA strong closing note reads: \"On Frame 4 I received an anchored comment about coherence overload. I accepted moving the paragraph to narration because it duplicated the diagram's point, and I rejected splitting the two diagrams because the objective is to compare them side by side. I logged both decisions with reasons; the rejection is the part that shows I weighed the comment against the objective rather than just obeying it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove peer review from a graduate cohort of near-equals to a workplace where a senior designer reviews a junior's storyboard. The three-part shape still holds, but the power gradient changes the authorship dynamic: an \"option\" from a senior reads as an order unless the senior is explicit that the junior owns the call, and a junior's principled rejection now carries professional risk. The transfer test is whether you can keep review anchored and authorship-preserving when the reviewer outranks the author, which usually means stating the anchor more, not less, explicitly and inviting the reasoned rejection on the record. If your review skill only works among equals, it collapses into compliance the moment a hierarchy is introduced; the durable skill is keeping the comment about the objective rather than about who outranks whom.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/03-07-peer-review-of-storyboards-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"03.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 03.07. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_peer_review_of_storyboards\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect peer review to the storyboarding work it audits, the evidence-based revision it triggers, and the bounded AI support whose suggestions it weighs.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\",\"peer_review\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_peer_review_of_storyboards","stem":"A peer review of a classmate's storyboard reads, in full: 'This looks great, nice work!' Why is this not yet peer review?","options":["It is too short to count as a sentence.","It offers no observation tied to a specific frame, principle, or learner outcome that the author could act on.","Praise is forbidden in graduate courses.","It does not mention the reviewer's name."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Review is actionable feedback anchored to specifics. General praise gives the author nothing to revise.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_praise_is_review","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["peer_review"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-01","ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-02","ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_peer_review_of_storyboards","stem":"A reviewer writes: 'I would have made the background blue and used a different font.' Why is this comment weak as design feedback?","options":["Blue is a poor color choice.","It rests on personal taste rather than the shared criteria, such as Mayer principles or the stated learning objective.","Reviewers may not comment on color at all.","Fonts are never part of a storyboard."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Useful feedback is anchored in the shared rubric and the objective. Preference comments do not tell the author whether a design standard was met or missed.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_review_taste_not_criteria","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["peer_review"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_peer_review_of_storyboards","stem":"While reviewing a peer's storyboard, you notice Frame 4 is overloaded. You ask an AI tool to help. Which use respects the author's authorship?","options":["Have the AI rewrite Frame 4 and paste its version into the peer's storyboard.","Use the AI to help you describe the overload precisely and name an option, then leave the revision choice to the author.","Ask the AI to grade the whole storyboard for the author.","Replace all of the peer's frames with AI-generated ones."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Review surfaces issues and options; it does not take over authorship. The reviewer (and any tool the reviewer uses) describes the problem and lets the author decide.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_reviewer_rewrites_artifact","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["peer_review"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_peer_review_of_storyboards_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_peer_review_of_storyboards","stem":"As the author, you receive eight peer comments, two of which contradict each other. What is the most defensible way to handle them?","options":["Apply all eight comments exactly as written.","Weigh each comment against the learning objective and constraints, then record which you accept, which you reject, and why.","Ignore all comments, since they conflict.","Apply only the comment from the highest-graded peer."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Feedback is input, not instruction. The author judges each comment against the objective and documents the accept or reject decision, which is the design evidence.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_accept_all_feedback","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["peer_review"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-07-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_praise_is_review","pattern":"encouragement_mistaken_for_review","description":"Treating general praise as peer review, rather than feedback tied to specific frames, principles, or learner outcomes","trigger":"reviewer writes looks great or nice work with no actionable observation","feedback":"Praise is not review. Point to a specific frame, name the principle at stake, and suggest a concrete change the author can act on.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_review_taste_not_criteria","pattern":"feedback_by_personal_taste","description":"Giving feedback from personal preference rather than against shared design criteria such as Mayer principles or the learning objective","trigger":"reviewer says I would have used blue instead of the rubric criteria","feedback":"Anchor feedback in the shared criteria and the learning objective, not personal style. Say which standard the frame meets or misses.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_reviewer_rewrites_artifact","pattern":"reviewer_takes_over_authorship","description":"Rewriting the author's storyboard during review instead of surfacing issues for the author to decide on","trigger":"reviewer replaces the author's frames with their own version","feedback":"Review surfaces issues and options; it does not seize authorship. Describe the problem and let the author choose the revision.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_7_accept_all_feedback","pattern":"author_accepts_feedback_uncritically","description":"As the author, accepting every reviewer comment without judging it against the learning goal and constraints","trigger":"author applies all comments, including conflicting ones, without deciding which serve the objective","feedback":"Feedback is input, not instruction. Weigh each comment against the objective and constraints; record which you accept, reject, and why.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"53bb07d38b3165cd2739467ff43cf1adb9fb87fcf267a7c9b726a2eb5acdfd68","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}